## Why This Is Asked

This question probes the ability to combine the Chain of Responsibility with a registry-driven plugin model, enabling runtime composition and reordering of middleware without touching route code.

## Key Concepts

- Chain of Responsibility
- Registry / Plugin architecture
- Dynamic composition and ordering
- Context mutation versus immutability

## Code Example

```typescript
type Context = { [k: string]: any };
type Next = () => Promise<void>;
type Middleware = (ctx: Context, next: Next) => Promise<void>;

class Registry {
  private store = new Map<string, Middleware[]>();
  register(name: string, mw: Middleware) {
    const arr = this.store.get(name) ?? [];
    arr.push(mw); this.store.set(name, arr);
  }
  get(name: string): Middleware[] | undefined {
    return this.store.get(name);
  }
}

function compose(list: Middleware[]): Middleware {
  return async (ctx, next) => {
    let i = -1;
    const dispatch = async (idx: number) => {
      if (idx < 0) return next();
      const mw = list[idx];
      if (!mw) return next();
      await mw(ctx, () => dispatch(idx + 1));
    };
    await dispatch(0);
  };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you handle middleware cancellation and timeouts without leaking resources?
- How would you test registry composition and ensure order guarantees across deployments?

## Why This Is Asked
This probes runtime extensibility, plugin patterns, and async composition for feature flags in multi-tenant systems.

## Key Concepts
- Registry-driven extensibility
- Rule composition with short-circuit evaluation
- Async and sync rule handling
- Per-tenant isolation via context
- Dynamic rule types (TimeWindow, UserAttribute, etc.)

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal TypeScript-like interfaces
type Context = { tenantId: string; userId?: string };
interface Rule { evaluate(ctx: Context): Promise<boolean>; }
type RuleFactory = (cfg?: any) => Rule;
class Registry { private m = new Map<string, RuleFactory>(); register(name, f){ this.m.set(name, f); } create(name, cfg){ const f = this.m.get(name); return f ? f(cfg) : undefined; } }
class TimeWindowRule implements Rule { constructor(start, end){ this.start=start; this.end=end; } async evaluate(ctx){ const t = Date.now()%86400000; return t>=this.start && t<=this.end; } }
class CompositeRule implements Rule { constructor(private rules: Rule[], private op: (a:boolean,b:boolean)=>boolean){ }
  async evaluate(ctx){ let acc = true; for (const r of this.rules){ const v = await r.evaluate(ctx); acc = this.op(acc, v); if(!acc) return false; } return acc; }
}
```

